<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nogent-le-Rotrou</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nogent-le-Rotrou is a town in the Eure-et-Loir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> on the Huisne, in the Perche region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It takes its name from the Rotrou counts of Perche, whose castle of Saint-Jean overlooks the town. In the 17th century it was bought by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilien-de-bethune-duke-of-sully">Maximilien de Béthune, Duke of Sully</a>, Henry IV's minister, who is buried there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nogent-le-rotrou.jpg" alt="Nogent-le-Rotrou" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roby · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20050921ChStJean1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
